\details{
Level 3 is the level at which the World Checklist of Vascular Plants records
distributions, so \verb{L3 code} is the key shared with \link{Distributions} and with
the polygon map in \link{WGSRPD3}. All 369 codes are present in \code{WGSRPD3}.

\verb{L3 ISOcode} is reproduced as published and is \strong{not} a complete or
one-to-one concordance. Three properties matter when using it to translate an
occurrence record's \code{countryCode} into candidate Level 3 areas:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Many areas share one country code.} A single ISO code usually maps to
several Level 3 units: \code{US} to 51, \code{RU} to 21, \code{CA} to 13, \code{CN} and \code{AU} to
8 each. Only 138 of the represented countries map to exactly one area, so
an ISO code generally identifies a \emph{set} of areas, not one area.
\item \strong{42 areas carry no ISO code at all}, among them the mainland units for
France (FRA), Italy (ITA), Spain (SPA), Austria (AUT), Belgium (BGM),
Ireland (IRE), Ukraine (UKR) and Korea (KOR). Because some offshore units
of the same countries \emph{do} carry a code, a naive lookup silently returns
the islands alone: \code{"FR"} resolves to Corse, \code{"IT"} to Sardegna, and
\code{"ES"} to the Baleares and Canary Islands. This yields a plausible but
wrong area rather than no answer.
\item \strong{One code is inconsistent with current usage.} Belarus (BLR) is listed
under ISO \code{RU}, reflecting the standard's 2001 vintage; records coded \code{BY}
therefore find no area, and records coded \code{RU} gain Belarus as a candidate.
}
